<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Gottfried Herder</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1744">1744</a>–<a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Herder</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Gottfried Herder was a German philosopher, theologian and writer, born in Mohrungen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>. A leading figure of the Sturm und Drang and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> Classicism, he argued that each people has its own language, culture and character, and he collected folk songs from many nations. From 1776 he was the chief Lutheran clergyman of Saxe-Weimar, where he worked alongside Goethe, Wieland and Schiller.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder.jpg" alt="Johann Gottfried Herder" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton Graff · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Gottfried_Herder_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
